This  driver  handles graphics hardware within the console frame‐
work.  It doesn’t provide direct device driver entry  points  but
makes  its  functions  available via the internal interface.  The
driver supports text‐mode hardware acceleration on the  hardware.
Currently, the driver runs the display with a 720×400 pixel reso‐
lution.   The  text‐mode  accelerator  divides  the  display into
fixed‐size character cells.  The  size  of  the  character  cells
specifies  the  number  of characters available on the screen and
the resolution of the font.  The wsdisplay  screen  supported  by
the  driver are described by the number of character cells avail‐
able on the screen.  See below for a complete list  of  supported
screen modes in the driver.  Each screen mode requires a suitable
font  to  be  loaded  into  the kernel by the utility, before the
screen can be used.  The size of the font  and  the  screen  mode
must match for use on the 720×400 display.  For example, a screen
mode with 80 columns and 40 rows requires a font where each char‐
acter  is  8 pixels wide and 10 pixels high.  The driver can dis‐
play fonts of the original type and ISO‐8859‐1 encoded fonts.   A
builtin  font of 256 characters and 8×16 pixels is always present
on the hardware.  The colour hardware supports the display of  16
different  colours  at the same time.  It is possible with colour
systems to use fonts with 512 characters at any one  time.   This
is  due to the fact that with VGA adapters one can specify an al‐
ternate font to be used instead of bright letters (used for high‐
lighting on the screen).  As an experimental feature,  the  fonts
of  the  former driver distribution can be used too if the kernel
option was set at compile time.  This is  only  useful  with  the
screen  types;  a font containing the range of characters must be
available too on this screen.  Currently,  the  following  screen
